{"title":"Abstract","markdown":{"headingText":"Abstract","headingAttr":{"id":"","classes":["unnumbered"],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nOpenPFC is a framework for solving partial differential equations on\nstructured, periodic, MPI-distributed grids, with matching CPU and GPU\nexecution paths. Its original target was the phase-field crystal (PFC) model,\nbut the architecture — a domain decomposition, a distributed field, a\npluggable spectral or finite-difference stack, and a session that wires them\nfrom JSON — is not specific to PFC.\n\nThis report describes the thirteen applications shipped with OpenPFC 0.2. They\nspan fourth- through tenth-order spatial operators, conserved and\nnon-conserved dynamics, dispersive and dissipative behaviour, single-field and\ncoupled multi-field systems, and both spectral and finite-difference\ndiscretisations.\n\nTwo claims run through the report.\n\nThe first is that **high differential order is not high implementation\ncomplexity in a spectral code**. A sixth-order elastohydrodynamic operator and\na fourth-order Cahn–Hilliard operator differ, in the source, by the polynomial\nreturned from one function. The equivalent real-space stencils would not be\ncomparable in effort. What high order does cost is stiffness, and the report\nis explicit about how exponential time differencing absorbs it.\n\nThe second is that **the same application source runs on a laptop core and on\n32 GPUs**. Chapter @sec-scalability reports measured strong-scaling curves for\nthree models on LUMI-G, out to 32 GCDs across four nodes, together with the\npoint at which each curve stops being worth extending.\n\nNeither claim is asserted without a number behind it. Where a property has not\nbeen measured, the report says so.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"defa